<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Larsa</h1><p class="st-meta">2025 BCE – 1763 BCE (approx.) · capital Larsa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Amorite kingdom of the sun god's city that dominated southern Mesopotamia under Rim-Sin before falling to Hammurabi of Babylon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Larsa was an old Sumerian city sacred to Utu (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>), whose temple was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar">E-babbar</a>. After the fall of Ur, an Amorite chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naplanum">Naplanum</a>, established himself there around 2025 BCE. Larsa long stood in the shadow of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isin">Isin</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungunum">Gungunum</a>, around 1932 BCE, seized Ur and its Gulf trade and claimed the old royal titles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Larsa's kings ruled an irrigated plain of barley, dates and sheep, controlled canals vital to rival cities and used Sumerian for royal inscriptions and Akkadian for letters and contracts. Around 1834 BCE an Amorite leader from Emutbal with an Elamite name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kudur-mabuk">Kudur-Mabuk</a>, installed his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warad-sin">Warad-Sin</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a> as kings. Rim-Sin's sixty-year reign was the longest in Mesopotamian history; he conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a> and in 1794 BCE took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isin">Isin</a>, uniting the south.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi of Babylon</a> besieged Larsa and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin</a> in 1763 BCE, absorbing the kingdom into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a>. Southern resentment surfaced later in revolts under Hammurabi's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a>, and the far south soon broke away under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sealand-dynasty">Sealand dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/larsa.jpg" alt="Larsa" width="320" height="279"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammurabi%27s_Babylonia_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2025 BCE – 1763 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Larsa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gungunum, Warad-Sin, Rim-Sin I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Larsa, Ellasar</dd></dl></aside></div>
